WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.734
Now games.

2
00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:07.320
A delivery driver picks one of two routes

3
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.654
North or South

4
00:00:08.654 --> 00:00:11.740
and an inspector checks one of them.

5
00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.298
The table gives the driver's gain

6
00:00:15.298 --> 00:00:17.941
time saved, minus any fine.

7
00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:20.677
North unchecked gains three

8
00:00:20.677 --> 00:00:23.414
but North checked loses two.

9
00:00:23.414 --> 00:00:25.961
South unchecked gains one

10
00:00:25.961 --> 00:00:28.321
South checked loses one.

11
00:00:28.321 --> 00:00:30.868
Whatever the driver gains

12
00:00:30.868 --> 00:00:32.850
the inspector loses.

13
00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.580
Suppose the driver commits to one route.

14
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.549
The inspector checks it.

15
00:00:38.628 --> 00:00:40.833
The best the driver can then

16
00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:43.510
guarantee is minus one, on South.

17
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.740
Suppose instead the inspector commits.

18
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.014
The driver avoids that route

19
00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:51.060
and the inspector can hold

20
00:00:51.136 --> 00:00:52.500
the driver to one.

21
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.091
Minus one against one: there is a gap.

22
00:00:56.091 --> 00:00:59.416
No single cell is best for both sides

23
00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:02.231
so the game has no saddle point

24
00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:05.130
and whoever is predictable loses.

25
00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.926
So the driver flips a weighted coin

26
00:01:08.926 --> 00:01:11.249
North with probability p.

27
00:01:11.249 --> 00:01:14.019
If the inspector checks North

28
00:01:14.019 --> 00:01:17.592
the expected gain is one minus three p.

29
00:01:17.592 --> 00:01:20.630
If South, it is four p minus one.

30
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.964
The inspector will choose

31
00:01:23.044 --> 00:01:24.869
the smaller of the two

32
00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:27.409
so the driver makes them equal.

33
00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:30.267
That gives p equal to two sevenths

34
00:01:30.346 --> 00:01:32.172
and an expected gain of

35
00:01:32.251 --> 00:01:33.918
one seventh per trip

36
00:01:33.918 --> 00:01:36.220
whatever the inspector does.

37
00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:37.726
In the same way

38
00:01:37.798 --> 00:01:40.037
the inspector checks North with

39
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.988
probability four sevenths

40
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.866
and that holds the driver

41
00:01:43.938 --> 00:01:45.599
to exactly one seventh.

42
00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:48.850
That common number is the value of the game.

43
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.097
Isn't mixing fifty fifty the safe

44
00:01:52.097 --> 00:01:53.430
cautious choice?

45
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.027
No.

46
00:01:54.027 --> 00:01:56.493
Against a fifty fifty driver

47
00:01:56.575 --> 00:01:59.123
checking North gives the driver

48
00:01:59.206 --> 00:02:01.343
minus one half on average.

49
00:02:01.343 --> 00:02:04.220
The inspector simply checks North.

50
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.805
The mix of two sevenths and five sevenths

51
00:02:07.884 --> 00:02:10.961
gives one seventh against both choices.

52
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.723
It is the only mix that leaves the

53
00:02:13.802 --> 00:02:16.090
inspector nothing to exploit.

54
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.248
This is always so.

55
00:02:18.248 --> 00:02:21.403
Von Neumann's minimax theorem says

56
00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:23.507
once mixing is allowed

57
00:02:23.594 --> 00:02:27.363
the best guarantee of one player equals the

58
00:02:27.451 --> 00:02:30.606
tightest limit the other can impose.

59
00:02:30.606 --> 00:02:32.710
Max min equals min max.

60
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.229
Like max flow and min cut

61
00:02:35.229 --> 00:02:38.900
it is a case of linear programming duality.

62
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.995
And when the best mix uses every row

63
00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:45.334
it has the same payoff against every column.

64
00:02:45.408 --> 00:02:48.079
Together with the condition that the

65
00:02:48.153 --> 00:02:50.230
probabilities add up to one

66
00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.604
that is a square linear system.

67
00:02:52.679 --> 00:02:55.127
Solving it gives the driver's mix

68
00:02:55.201 --> 00:02:57.130
and the value of the game.

69
00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.033
Now a game of our own: rock, paper

70
00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:03.786
scissors with uneven prizes.

71
00:03:03.786 --> 00:03:07.014
Rock beats scissors and wins one.

72
00:03:07.014 --> 00:03:10.337
Scissors beats paper and wins two.

73
00:03:10.337 --> 00:03:13.470
Paper beats rock and wins three.

74
00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.807
The payoff matrix is antisymmetric

75
00:03:16.807 --> 00:03:20.220
so the game is fair: its value is zero.

76
00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:23.300
Paper's win is worth the most.

77
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.303
How often should you play paper?

78
00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:27.850
Pause and guess.

79
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.722
Least often: one sixth of the time.

80
00:03:34.722 --> 00:03:38.390
Rock one third, and scissors one half.

81
00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.279
With this mix, every reply of the opponent

82
00:03:42.363 --> 00:03:44.216
is worth exactly zero

83
00:03:44.301 --> 00:03:46.997
so the mix lies in the nullspace

84
00:03:47.081 --> 00:03:48.345
of G transpose.

85
00:03:48.345 --> 00:03:50.114
Why so little paper?

86
00:03:50.198 --> 00:03:52.389
The mix is set by what the

87
00:03:52.473 --> 00:03:56.180
opponent could gain, not by your own prizes.

88
00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.099
And the even mix, a third each

89
00:03:59.182 --> 00:04:02.746
loses a third per round against an opponent

90
00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:05.730
who answers with paper or scissors.

91
00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.720
Not every game needs a mix.

92
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.844
In the game three, one, five, two

93
00:04:11.844 --> 00:04:14.700
the row minima are one and two

94
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.270
and the column maxima are five and two.

95
00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.385
Both meet at two: a saddle point.

96
00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:23.732
Both players play one fixed

97
00:04:23.816 --> 00:04:25.324
choice every time.

98
00:04:25.408 --> 00:04:28.173
The equalising system now returns

99
00:04:28.257 --> 00:04:30.687
a mix of three and minus two

100
00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:32.447
which is impossible.

101
00:04:32.447 --> 00:04:35.716
When some rows should never be played

102
00:04:35.716 --> 00:04:38.900
a single linear system is not enough.

103
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.015
Working with AI.

104
00:04:41.111 --> 00:04:44.373
Suppose an assistant averages each

105
00:04:44.469 --> 00:04:48.499
row of the driver's table: North one half

106
00:04:48.499 --> 00:04:52.720
South zero, and advises: always take North.

107
00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:55.205
Averages assume the inspector

108
00:04:55.279 --> 00:04:56.604
chooses at random.

109
00:04:56.678 --> 00:04:58.960
A driver who always takes North

110
00:04:59.034 --> 00:05:00.654
is checked every time

111
00:05:00.654 --> 00:05:02.421
and loses two per trip.

112
00:05:02.494 --> 00:05:04.777
Test any strategy against every

113
00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:06.470
reply of the opponent.

114
00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:07.817
To sum up.

115
00:05:07.817 --> 00:05:09.658
Without a saddle point

116
00:05:09.735 --> 00:05:12.343
the best plan is a mix that leaves

117
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.338
the opponent indifferent.

118
00:05:14.338 --> 00:05:16.179
When every row is used

119
00:05:16.179 --> 00:05:18.250
it solves a linear system.

120
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.871
Max min equals min max

121
00:05:20.969 --> 00:05:23.338
just as the maximum flow

122
00:05:23.437 --> 00:05:25.708
equals the minimum cut.

123
00:05:25.708 --> 00:05:29.360
Both are linear programming duality.

124
00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:30.797
Now you try

125
00:05:30.888 --> 00:05:34.420
find the inspector's mix by solving the

126
00:05:34.511 --> 00:05:37.590
same system for minus G transpose.

127
00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.319
For linear programs in general

128
00:05:40.396 --> 00:05:42.621
follow the link on page E2 to

129
00:05:42.698 --> 00:05:44.540
the optimisation course.
